#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e66664f5-380e-5745-be2c-90ad323f2dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{46}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279160} {:answer/id #uuid "0714c6d5-fa2e-5d2a-b410-9cc7f6f50f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279161} {:answer/id #uuid "726f5315-901e-5c3f-a2a2-290b90bc03b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279162} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc7251c-e514-5ea7-bf1a-270ab49b97f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279163} {:answer/id #uuid "0adb7bb9-01cb-5473-8b8b-f7b52effa7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0714c6d5-fa2e-5d2a-b410-9cc7f6f50f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279161} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5b0741-3fca-5a95-8d71-75222a5d3195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2793518d-ad57-5b32-92b4-c966424db7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124813" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,2)$, $B(3,-5,1)$, and $C(1,-4,-1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,2⟩,\\,b=⟨3,-5,1⟩,\\,c=⟨1,-4,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,1⟩-⟨2,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,-1⟩-⟨2,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3\\sqrt{38}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{14}{3\\sqrt{38}})≈{41}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aca6bd26-6933-59e2-8800-0702ffaaee5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102430} {:answer/id #uuid "b59b3837-6fdf-509f-b5ae-fc2925a1d015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102431} {:answer/id #uuid "bd485a1b-1304-5ab4-ac9e-8d4a2f9d2b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102432} {:answer/id #uuid "08d4a2e8-a01f-5019-a296-05c07d911af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102433} {:answer/id #uuid "673c1a77-a57c-5bbd-8e96-8088500a00a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08d4a2e8-a01f-5019-a296-05c07d911af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102433} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2546906-9052-54aa-8870-1c81e7a0a86b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6f80f82-94f9-5bde-ac4d-44929ef66109" :question/math-academy-id "q-19110" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0,4,5)$, $Q(1,5,4)$, and $R(2,3,6).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,5,4⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
